[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      4[0m:[38;5;10m[49m executables and diffing stdout, stderr and exit status.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      5[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      6[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Headline result
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      7[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      8[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **No behavioral mismatch was found between the two programs.** Across 1740
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      9[0m:[38;5;10m[49m differential cases in `tests/differential.rs`, plus a further ~15,000 ad-hoc and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     10[0m:[38;5;10m[49m randomized cases run during investigation, stdout, stderr and exit status were
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     11[0m:[38;5;10m[49m byte-identical on every input.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     12[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     13[0m:[38;5;10m[49m One real defect *was* found and fixed — in the test suite, not in the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     14[0m:[38;5;10m[49m translation. It is recorded in "Defect 1" below, because a gap that lets a wrong
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     15[0m:[38;5;10m[49m translation pass is the same class of problem as a wrong translation.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     16[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Build and run commands
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | | command | binary |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | C | `cd c_src && mkdir -p build && cd build && cmake .. && cmake --build .` | `c_src/build/driver` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Rust | `cd translation && cargo build --release` | `translation/target/release/driver` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Both build clean, no errors and no warnings. `cargo test` builds the C binary
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m itself (guarded by a `OnceLock` so parallel test threads invoke CMake once), so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the suite is self-contained.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Branches enumerated from the C source
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `main`:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **M0** `scanf` completes 0 conversions → `x=0`, `y=123`, `z=0`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **M1** completes 1 → `x` set, `y` stays at its initialiser 123, `z=0`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **M2** completes 2 → `x`, `y` set, `z=0`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **M3** completes 3
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **M4** `printf("Result: %d\n", result)` then `return 0`, on every path
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `multi_stage(int x, int z)` — note `y` is the file-scope global read directly,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m not a parameter:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **S1** `x != 1` → `Error: x != 1`, result 1, `goto fail`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **S2** `x == 1 && y != 2` → `Error: x == 1 but y != 2`, result 2, `goto fail`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **S3** `x == 1 && y == 2 && z != 3` → `Error: x == 1 and y == 2, but z != 3`, result 3, `goto fail`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **S4** all three match → `Ok!` and `return` **before** the `fail:` label, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `Operation failed` is not printed. This is the only path that skips it.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **S5** the `fail:` label → `Operation failed`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Each branch has a named test. `full_truth_table_across_separators` additionally
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m crosses x ∈ {1,2,0,-1} × y ∈ {2,3,0,-2} × z ∈ {3,4,0,-3} × 5 separator styles so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m no ordering of the three checks can hide behind another.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Defect 1 — test suite could not distinguish 32-bit from 16-bit truncation
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Severity: real. Found by mutation testing, now fixed.**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `scanf("%d", …)` stores through an `int *`, so the parsed value is truncated to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m exactly 32 bits. The original suite covered truncation using values of the form
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `k·2³² + 1`. Those values have the same low 16 bits *and* low 32 bits, so they
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m cannot tell the widths apart.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Demonstrated by mutating `Some(acc as i32)` to `Some(acc as i16 as i32)`: the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m mutant passed the entire suite. It is genuinely wrong — for input `65537 2 3`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (`0x00010001`) the C prints `Error: x != 1` while the i16 mutant prints `Ok!`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m because `65537 as i16 == 1`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Fix: added `truncation_width_is_exactly_32_bits`, which sweeps values whose low
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 8 bits and low 16 bits alias 1/2/3 without the `int` value doing so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (`k·256 + {1,2,3}`, `k·65536 + {1,2,3}`), their negative counterparts
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (`-65535`, `-255`, …), and the sign-extension boundaries 127/128/255/256/
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 32767/32768/65535/65536 in each of the three token positions. The i8, i16 and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m "no truncation" mutants are all killed now.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Behaviors that were verified rather than assumed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m These are the places a plausible translation would have diverged. Each was
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m checked against the compiled C program, not against the C standard.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### `%d` overflow saturates, it does not wrap
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m glibc's `%d` accumulates the digit run and converts it with `strtol`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m semantics, which **saturates** at `LONG_MAX`/`LONG_MIN` on overflow; the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m saturated `long` is then truncated on the store to `int`. This is observably
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m different from 64-bit wrapping arithmetic:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | input | C output | wrapping would give |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `1 4294967298 3` (2³²+2, fits in `long`) | `Ok!` — truncates to y=2 | same |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `1 18446744073709551618 3` (2⁶⁴+2) | `Error: x == 1 but y != 2` — saturates to `LONG_MAX`, y = −1 | y = 2 → `Ok!` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `1 -18446744073709551614 3` (−(2⁶⁴−2)) | y error — saturates to `LONG_MIN`, y = 0 | y = 2 → `Ok!` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `translation/src/main.rs` implements exactly this (saturating `i64`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m accumulation, then `as i32`), and matches. Covered by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `overflow_saturation_and_truncation` and `low_32_bits_sweep`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Note this behavior depends on `long` being 64-bit. On an ILP32 target the C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m program itself would behave differently; the translation matches the LP64 build
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m that `c_src/CMakeLists.txt` produces here.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### `%d` crosses newlines
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `%d` skips arbitrary leading whitespace including `\n`, so `1\n2\n3\n`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `1\n\n\n2\n\n\n3\n` and 10,000 interleaved newlines all reach the success path.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m A `fgets`-style line-at-a-time reader would have failed these. Verified by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m mutating the whitespace predicate to exclude `\n` — killed.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The whitespace set is C's `isspace` in the "C" locale exactly: space, `\t`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `\n`, `\v` (0x0b), `\f` (0x0c), `\r`. Bytes 0x1c–0x1f are **not** whitespace and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m terminate a token; `non_ascii_and_control_bytes` pins this down.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### `strtol` base-10 token termination
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `0x10` parses as `0` and stops at `x`; `1e5` parses as `1`; `1.5` parses as `1`;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `1_2` parses as `1`; `010` parses as ten, not eight. All covered by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `strtol_token_termination`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### A sign with no digits is a matching failure, not zero
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `-`, `+`, `- 1`, `--1`, `+-1` leave the target variable untouched rather than
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m setting it to 0. See "Equivalent mutants" below for why this turns out to be
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m unobservable in *this* program even though the translation implements it
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m correctly.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### Arbitrarily long digit runs
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m A 100,001-digit token is accepted and converted (leading zeros do not count
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m toward overflow). `very_long_digit_runs` covers 5,001-digit and 100,001-digit
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m tokens; both programs agree.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### Exit status is always 0, stderr is always empty
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The C `main` ends in `return 0` unconditionally, on every error path. Every
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m error message goes to **stdout**, not stderr. A translation that mapped result
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 1/2/3 onto the process exit code would pass a stdout-only comparison and fail
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m here — verified by mutating the Rust to `exit(result)`, which is killed.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### I/O failure paths
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    138[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - stdout redirected to `/dev/full` (writes fail with ENOSPC): the C ignores every
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    139[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `printf` return value and still exits 0. The Rust `let _ = write!(…)` matches.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   Mutating those to `.unwrap()` is killed.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    141[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - stdin closed, stdin `/dev/null`, stdin a directory (`read` → EISDIR): all
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    142[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   produce the M0/S1 path identically in both.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    143[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Up to 1 MiB of unread trailing stdin: both programs exit without draining it,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    144[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   and the test harness tolerates the resulting `EPIPE` on its writer thread.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    145[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    146[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### argv is ignored
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    147[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    148[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `main()` takes no parameters. `argv_is_ignored` confirms extra arguments change
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    149[0m:[38;5;10m[49m nothing in either program.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    150[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    151[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Equivalent mutants — unobservable, not untested
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    152[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    153[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Two deliberate defects survived the suite. Both were investigated and shown to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    154[0m:[38;5;10m[49m be **behaviorally equivalent to the C program**, so no test could have killed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    155[0m:[38;5;10m[49m them:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    156[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    157[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 1. a lone sign yielding `Some(0)` instead of a matching failure
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    158[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 2. `scanf` continuing past a failed conversion instead of stopping at the first
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    159[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    160[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Evidence: each mutant was built and compared against the C binary over 13,313
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    161[0m:[38;5;10m[49m inputs — an exhaustive enumeration of all strings of length ≤ 4 over the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    162[0m:[38;5;10m[49m class-covering alphabet `{1,2,3,-,+,space,x,0,\n}`, plus targeted lone-sign
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    163[0m:[38;5;10m[49m inputs in all three token positions, plus 5,500 randomized cases. Zero
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    164[0m:[38;5;10m[49m distinguishing inputs.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    165[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    166[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The reason is structural: a failed conversion can only change a variable that
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    167[0m:[38;5;10m[49m either already fails its own check, or is never reached. `x != 1` and `y != 2`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    168[0m:[38;5;10m[49m each return before `z` is examined, and if `x` and `y` both converted
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    169[0m:[38;5;10m[49m successfully then a failure can only occur at `z`, where there is nothing left
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    170[0m:[38;5;10m[49m to continue to. Likewise `x`'s and `z`'s initialisers are both 0, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    171[0m:[38;5;10m[49m "untouched" and "set to 0" are indistinguishable, and `y`'s initialiser 123 and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    172[0m:[38;5;10m[49m a mutant's 0 are both `!= 2`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    173[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    174[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The translation implements the correct (C-faithful) behavior in both cases
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    175[0m:[38;5;10m[49m anyway; this note exists so a future reader does not mistake the survival for a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    176[0m:[38;5;10m[49m coverage hole.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    177[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    178[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Also unobservable: `y`'s initialiser value
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    179[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    180[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `static int y = 123;` is only ever compared against 2, and the only input class
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    181[0m:[38;5;10m[49m that leaves it at 123 (M1) reports `y != 2` regardless. Changing 123 to any
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    182[0m:[38;5;10m[49m value other than 2 produces identical output on all inputs. Changing it *to* 2
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    183[0m:[38;5;10m[49m is observable and is killed by `m1_one_conversion` (input `1` → `Ok!` instead of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    184[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the y error).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    185[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    186[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Known divergence outside the compared surface: SIGPIPE
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    187[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    188[0m:[38;5;10m[49m A Rust program starts with `SIGPIPE` set to `SIG_IGN`; a C program does not. If
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    189[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stdout were a pipe whose reader had already exited, the C program would die with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    190[0m:[38;5;10m[49m signal 13 where the Rust program would exit 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    191[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    192[0m:[38;5;10m[49m This is **not reachable for this program**: its entire output is at most 60
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    193[0m:[38;5;10m[49m bytes, which always fits in a pipe buffer, so `write` never returns `EPIPE`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    194[0m:[38;5;10m[49m before the process exits. Tested with `… | head -c 1`: both exit 0. It is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    195[0m:[38;5;10m[49m recorded here for completeness rather than fixed, since suppressing it would
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    196[0m:[38;5;10m[49m require an `unsafe` `signal(2)` call for a path no input can reach.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    197[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    198[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mutation testing summary
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    199[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
